(1831797, 248107, 29904, '<td valign="top">This is absolutly magnificent!  I am ashamed for my fellow voters that were stupid enough to give it anything less than a 5, all 59 of them!  Any that had qualms about the Light on White need to think of the reason for the exercise, which is to excellently photograph almost white (on) white without loosing form, or dulling or blowing out the brighter end of the tones. The less color the greater the challenge.  You dared to shoot ALL white and did so beautifully!<br/>Love how neat image adds a dreamy ''marble-like'' feel, as sglantz put it.</td>', 'This is absolutly magnificent!  I am ashamed for my fellow voters that were stupid enough to give it anything less than a 5, all 59 of them!  Any that had qualms about the Light on White need to think of the reason for the exercise, which is to excellently photograph almost white (on) white without loosing form, or dulling or blowing out the brighter end of the tones. The less color the greater the challenge.  You dared to shoot ALL white and did so beautifully!Love how neat image adds a dreamy ''marble-like'' feel, as sglantz put it.', '2005-11-02 01:24:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(1831799, 249203, 28882, '<td valign="top">i''m amazed.</td>', 'i''m amazed.', '2005-11-02 01:24:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(1831802, 249392, 32602, '<td valign="top">she looks like she stepped out of a thirties musical or a sixties sci-fi film, very nice</td>', 'she looks like she stepped out of a thirties musical or a sixties sci-fi film, very nice', '2005-11-02 01:25:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(1831807, 248963, 21216, '<td valign="top">Magnificent picture! They stole the ribbon from you.</td>', 'Magnificent picture! They stole the ribbon from you.', '2005-11-02 01:27:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(1831809, 249555, 46601, '<td valign="top">Received a few questions as to how I did this one.  First of all the light bulb is ON and is connected to wires.  If you look closely at the base you can see a very small fine silver wire wrapped around the last thread ... this is the neutral wire.  The other wire is soldered to the very tip of the base (you can see the little bump) .. this is the live wire.  <br/> <br/>After attaching the wires to the bulb I hung it upside down, using the wire to suspend it at a 90 degree angle (the gauge of wire that I used was VERY heavy, which allowed for them to support the weight of the bulb at the required 90 degree angle).  I placed the wires through a piece of white paper and then wired the whole thing up to AC!  (Warning do not try to do this at home if you don''t know what you are doing!)  <br/> <br/>Took the photo at f4.0 and 1/500 ... used photoshop to lighten the image a little to blend a little of the bulb in with the background, making it look very "bright".<br/> <br/>Thanks for all your comments!</td>', 'Received a few questions as to how I did this one.  First of all the light bulb is ON and is connected to wires.  If you look closely at the base you can see a very small fine silver wire wrapped around the last thread ... this is the neutral wire.  The other wire is soldered to the very tip of the base (you can see the little bump) .. this is the live wire.   After attaching the wires to the bulb I hung it upside down, using the wire to suspend it at a 90 degree angle (the gauge of wire that I used was VERY heavy, which allowed for them to support the weight of the bulb at the required 90 degree angle).  I placed the wires through a piece of white paper and then wired the whole thing up to AC!  (Warning do not try to do this at home if you don''t know what you are doing!)   Took the photo at f4.0 and 1/500 ... used photoshop to lighten the image a little to blend a little of the bulb in with the background, making it look very "bright". Thanks for all your comments!', '2005-11-02 01:28:01.000000', NULL, 0),
