(1837594, 249600, 37719, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>So we meet again...first - if you have any problems with me giving you another critique so soon, please contact me after and I will talk to the members and see if we can get someone else to critique this again.<br/> <br/>Composition/perspective - this is a very simple image compositionally - not busy, no clutter, well defined subjects.  The placement of the eggs is very well done.  They fill enough of the image without being overbearing or crowding the negative space. Having only the center egg completely in the shot really works very well for this.  I think the top down view also works well especially with your choice of lighting.<br/> <br/>Lighting - eggs have a lot of natural texture on the surface.  Little bumps and such.  You can see this only on the top edges of the top two eggs.  A little bright in my opinion.  Slightly less and that texture would be clearly evident while not hurting the strength of the image at all.  The angle of the light is excellent and the control of the shadows is very well done.  Based on the shadows it appears as only one light source was used??  Perhaps with a second you could lessent the intensity of the one that is taking away the texture.  You might lose some of the shadow, but I don''t think that would have hurt.  Overall though, white on white is not easy to shoot and your overall control of the lighting is very well done. <br/> <br/>Color - again, white on white is not easy.  It is so easy to lose definition of shape.  This is not the case here.  The small shadows help keep the subjects isolated from the background and all edges are clearly evident and well defined.<br/> <br/>Challenge requirements - no doubt you have met the challenge.  And quite effefctively at that.<br/> <br/>Overall/my opinion - this is a very well done image demonstrating many techiques all of which you seem to be on top of.  The simplicity of the subject matter is very pleasing.  It is not a very emotive piece, but it doesn''t really have to be.  The crop really works well.  It may not have needed the border, but keeping it simple and thin keeps it from being a distration or a detractor.</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! So we meet again...first - if you have any problems with me giving you another critique so soon, please contact me after and I will talk to the members and see if we can get someone else to critique this again. Composition/perspective - this is a very simple image compositionally - not busy, no clutter, well defined subjects.  The placement of the eggs is very well done.  They fill enough of the image without being overbearing or crowding the negative space. Having only the center egg completely in the shot really works very well for this.  I think the top down view also works well especially with your choice of lighting. Lighting - eggs have a lot of natural texture on the surface.  Little bumps and such.  You can see this only on the top edges of the top two eggs.  A little bright in my opinion.  Slightly less and that texture would be clearly evident while not hurting the strength of the image at all.  The angle of the light is excellent and the control of the shadows is very well done.  Based on the shadows it appears as only one light source was used??  Perhaps with a second you could lessent the intensity of the one that is taking away the texture.  You might lose some of the shadow, but I don''t think that would have hurt.  Overall though, white on white is not easy to shoot and your overall control of the lighting is very well done.  Color - again, white on white is not easy.  It is so easy to lose definition of shape.  This is not the case here.  The small shadows help keep the subjects isolated from the background and all edges are clearly evident and well defined. Challenge requirements - no doubt you have met the challenge.  And quite effefctively at that. Overall/my opinion - this is a very well done image demonstrating many techiques all of which you seem to be on top of.  The simplicity of the subject matter is very pleasing.  It is not a very emotive piece, but it doesn''t really have to be.  The crop really works well.  It may not have needed the border, but keeping it simple and thin keeps it from being a distration or a detractor.', '2005-11-04 09:04:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(1837648, 248931, 37719, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Composition/perspective - your angle of approach to this shot is very good.  Low, but not too low that the dog appears flat.  Your subject is very centered, perhaps placing him off to one side or more towards a corner would make it a stronger image.  The subject itself is strong enough that applying the rule of thirds would not have hurt the image.  The negative space also would not have hurt if it was weighted to one side.  The face appears a bit soft and the paws appear slighty fuzzy.  Perhaps application of USM or a different focus would eliminate that.<br/> <br/>Lighting - overall the lighting is well done.  A few areas seem a bit blown out - from the top of the head to the middle of the back for example.  You start to lose the definition of his back in the brightness and there azre areas where the subject is indistinguishable from the background.  Control of the shadows is well done.  Enough to keep the subject well defined in most areas, not too much to give dark spots.<br/> <br/>Color - light on white obviously by definition does not contain a lot of color variation.  The skin tone and spots showing on the subject really help to keep the white coat from getting totally lost in the background.<br/> <br/>Challenge requirements - you obviously have met the challenge very well.    You have a strong subject who seems to not mind posing for you.<br/> <br/>Overall/my opinion - this is a very pleasing portrait.  It could be a stronger image if composed slightly differently.  But overall you did a very nice job both with your image and meeting the challenge.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! Composition/perspective - your angle of approach to this shot is very good.  Low, but not too low that the dog appears flat.  Your subject is very centered, perhaps placing him off to one side or more towards a corner would make it a stronger image.  The subject itself is strong enough that applying the rule of thirds would not have hurt the image.  The negative space also would not have hurt if it was weighted to one side.  The face appears a bit soft and the paws appear slighty fuzzy.  Perhaps application of USM or a different focus would eliminate that. Lighting - overall the lighting is well done.  A few areas seem a bit blown out - from the top of the head to the middle of the back for example.  You start to lose the definition of his back in the brightness and there azre areas where the subject is indistinguishable from the background.  Control of the shadows is well done.  Enough to keep the subject well defined in most areas, not too much to give dark spots. Color - light on white obviously by definition does not contain a lot of color variation.  The skin tone and spots showing on the subject really help to keep the white coat from getting totally lost in the background. Challenge requirements - you obviously have met the challenge very well.    You have a strong subject who seems to not mind posing for you. Overall/my opinion - this is a very pleasing portrait.  It could be a stronger image if composed slightly differently.  But overall you did a very nice job both with your image and meeting the challenge. ', '2005-11-04 09:52:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(1837712, 249604, 43437, '<td valign="top">thank you for the comments, I did mess up re-sizing this photo and since I entered it in the last 2 minuites when I noticed it I didnt have time to fix my error.  I loved this picture though.  THanks for the comments</td>', 'thank you for the comments, I did mess up re-sizing this photo and since I entered it in the last 2 minuites when I noticed it I didnt have time to fix my error.  I loved this picture though.  THanks for the comments', '2005-11-04 10:46:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(1837716, 248827, 37719, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>After studying your image I can say that I think the idea and execution of that idea are in conflict.<br/> <br/>Composition/perspective - the heart is very centered in the image which takes away from the potential it has.  Also it is very straight, as in the safety pin and beads are at a nice angle but the heart is just flat.  A slight angle to the heart would help here as well as placintg the subject off to one side weighting the negative space.  By flat I mean that the heart just seems to be lying/hanging there.  Perhaps angling your camera to offset that would help - 80 degrees to the subject or so.  This would give the heart some depth.  The focus is off.  Nothing seems to be the center of focus or attention of the camera.  Again, the subject is just there.<br/> <br/>Lighting - with no distinct reflections on the image it gives me the appearance that it is backlit.  The background seems a little too bright.  There are some streaks in the background as well.  Not sure if the lighting caused this or post processing.  But they do present some distraction.  The edges of the heart also seem to flare a bit, again giving me the impression of backlighting (which is a good technique) but just a bit too bright.<br/> <br/>Color - the red of the heart is a bit dark for this challenge.  A softer pink may have worked better. In a different challenge this would be good - it is a nice rich color that is strong and could be used quite effectively.  The white background is not all white.  There are streaks of red and yellow across the image that should have been removed.<br/> <br/>Challenge requirements - light on white is not an easy topic.  It is very difficult under optimum conditions.  However, here I think the red is just too strong.  The beads and pin are ok since they are on top and the heart dominates the space.  The background also takes away from conformance since it is not all white, but streaked as stated above.<br/> <br/>Overall/my opinion - the concept you have here is a good one.  I think it fails in the composition though.  With slightly different lighting and a better focus this would be a stronger image.  With slightly different coloring in the heart definitely would have helped.  Your creativity shows with this image and I think you are demonstrating your potential.  A little more work on composition and execution and you will have it down.</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! After studying your image I can say that I think the idea and execution of that idea are in conflict. Composition/perspective - the heart is very centered in the image which takes away from the potential it has.  Also it is very straight, as in the safety pin and beads are at a nice angle but the heart is just flat.  A slight angle to the heart would help here as well as placintg the subject off to one side weighting the negative space.  By flat I mean that the heart just seems to be lying/hanging there.  Perhaps angling your camera to offset that would help - 80 degrees to the subject or so.  This would give the heart some depth.  The focus is off.  Nothing seems to be the center of focus or attention of the camera.  Again, the subject is just there. Lighting - with no distinct reflections on the image it gives me the appearance that it is backlit.  The background seems a little too bright.  There are some streaks in the background as well.  Not sure if the lighting caused this or post processing.  But they do present some distraction.  The edges of the heart also seem to flare a bit, again giving me the impression of backlighting (which is a good technique) but just a bit too bright. Color - the red of the heart is a bit dark for this challenge.  A softer pink may have worked better. In a different challenge this would be good - it is a nice rich color that is strong and could be used quite effectively.  The white background is not all white.  There are streaks of red and yellow across the image that should have been removed. Challenge requirements - light on white is not an easy topic.  It is very difficult under optimum conditions.  However, here I think the red is just too strong.  The beads and pin are ok since they are on top and the heart dominates the space.  The background also takes away from conformance since it is not all white, but streaked as stated above. Overall/my opinion - the concept you have here is a good one.  I think it fails in the composition though.  With slightly different lighting and a better focus this would be a stronger image.  With slightly different coloring in the heart definitely would have helped.  Your creativity shows with this image and I think you are demonstrating your potential.  A little more work on composition and execution and you will have it down.', '2005-11-04 10:48:15.000000', NULL, 0),
