(4089764, 249472, 43463, '<td valign="top">Now that I am not obsessing over several challenge entries I wanted to stop by and leave a comment on this one that you posted in the thread titled "Why oh WHY can''t it be easy?". As quite a few pointed out that the bg is too gray rather than the bright white I think you were going for. I like the idea but a bright white background and sharper detail on the light bulb would help this compositional idea tremendously. You might want to try building a simple &amp; easy <a href="//www.diyphotography.net/homestudio/super-simple-light-tent" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Super Simple Light Tent</a>. Playing around with a smaller aperature (6.3 or higher) and slower shutter speed (1/10 to 1/60)will net you sharper details in your main subject and quite possibly get a brighter white depending on what background you use &amp; lighting you use (be careful of the tungsten light bulbs for they can give a yellowish cast to the image). Another thing that I found instrumental is adjusting the WB on your camera. For a recent shoot I changed the White Balance from Auto to Custom and found that it got rid of the awful yellow tinge or flat tinge in the photo.</td>', 'Now that I am not obsessing over several challenge entries I wanted to stop by and leave a comment on this one that you posted in the thread titled "Why oh WHY can''t it be easy?". As quite a few pointed out that the bg is too gray rather than the bright white I think you were going for. I like the idea but a bright white background and sharper detail on the light bulb would help this compositional idea tremendously. You might want to try building a simple & easy Super Simple Light Tent. Playing around with a smaller aperature (6.3 or higher) and slower shutter speed (1/10 to 1/60)will net you sharper details in your main subject and quite possibly get a brighter white depending on what background you use & lighting you use (be careful of the tungsten light bulbs for they can give a yellowish cast to the image). Another thing that I found instrumental is adjusting the WB on your camera. For a recent shoot I changed the White Balance from Auto to Custom and found that it got rid of the awful yellow tinge or flat tinge in the photo.', '2007-09-14 12:25:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(4528003, 249577, 88136, '<td valign="top">i think Membermacrothing was hypercritical of your photo.  i think its wonderful, i love you focus and everything else as well.  i can never seem to get good pictures of my chicks cause the hens freak out too bad for me to take them away for a little bit. and the chicks are super quick runners</td>', 'i think Membermacrothing was hypercritical of your photo.  i think its wonderful, i love you focus and everything else as well.  i can never seem to get good pictures of my chicks cause the hens freak out too bad for me to take them away for a little bit. and the chicks are super quick runners', '2008-02-29 12:58:26.000000', NULL, 0),
